---
import { Activity, ArrowDown, ArrowRight, ArrowUpRight, Boxes, Check, ChevronDown, Code, Cpu, Database, HardDrive, Layers, MessageSquare, Radio, SlidersHorizontal, Smartphone } from '@lucide/astro';
import Face from '../components/Face.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import release from '../data/alpha-release.json';

const stages = [
  {
    id: 'discover', number: '01', name: 'Find your next model', short: 'Discover', icon: Database,
    description: 'Start with a model recommended for the machine you choose. See which versions fit its available memory, at a stated context length, before downloading. Search the catalogue or paste a Hugging Face link for more options.',
    output: 'A starting point for your hardware', repo: 'library',
  },
  {
    id: 'configure', number: '02', name: 'Make the setup yours', short: 'Configure', icon: SlidersHorizontal,
    description: 'Choose a passphrase and a model folder in a two-screen setup. Existing files are scanned where they are. Settings start at a conversation length that fits your machine. You can save answer settings for each model too; settings sent by your app win.',
    output: 'Suggested settings you can change', repo: 'ui',
  },
  {
    id: 'runtimes', number: '03', name: 'From download to first reply', short: 'Run', icon: Cpu,
    description: 'Download and run is one action. Install llama.cpp with your approval, launch the model, and try a first reply right away. The agent restarts crashed engines; the gateway can unload idle models and wake them on demand.',
    output: 'Engine setup and launch together', repo: 'agent',
  },
  {
    id: 'routing', number: '04', name: 'One address, more options', short: 'Route', icon: Radio,
    description: 'Connect OpenAI-compatible tools or Claude Code to one address, using revocable client keys. Keep Ollama or LM Studio beside the engines Eugene manages, cloud services, and Claude Code or Codex CLI subscriptions. Work is shared across copies of a model, and moves to your next choice when one fails.',
    output: 'OpenAI and Anthropic Messages APIs', repo: 'gateway',
  },
  {
    id: 'observe', number: '05', name: 'See what actually happened', short: 'Observe', icon: Activity,
    description: 'Track downloads and model loading in the tasks tray. See problems that need attention, look back at requests and failed attempts, or use the Playground to see exactly what your apps send and get back.',
    output: 'Status, problems, and request history', repo: 'ui',
  },
];

// What a casual visitor came to do, in their words. Each line is true of
// the shipped alpha; the checks behind them are the acceptance records.
const uses = [
  {
    icon: MessageSquare, title: 'Chat privately on your PC',
    text: 'Download a model that fits, then talk to it in your browser. What you type stays on your computer.',
  },
  {
    icon: Smartphone, title: 'Use it from your phone',
    text: 'Turn on Reach, then open the address it shows. It works on your home Wi-Fi, or on a private network such as Tailscale.',
  },
  {
    icon: Code, title: 'Power your coding tools',
    text: 'Point Claude Code, Continue or Cline at a model on your own graphics card. Each app gets its own key, and you can turn any key off.',
  },
  {
    icon: Boxes, title: 'Keep what you already run',
    text: 'Already use Ollama or LM Studio? Add them beside the models Eugene runs, and give every app the same address.',
  },
];

// A question is a lower bar than a bug report, so it gets its own door,
// with the title started for them. GitHub still needs an account.
const askUrl = 'https://github.com/eugene-plexus/specs/issues/new?title=Question%3A%20';

// The install page's "Before you start", cut to what decides the answer.
const requirements = [
  { term: 'Computer', text: 'Windows or Linux, 64-bit. Mac is still being tested.' },
  { term: 'Memory', text: '16 GB of RAM for a small first model. Bigger models need more.' },
  { term: 'Disk space', text: '10 GB free to start. Most models are several gigabytes each.' },
  { term: 'Graphics card', text: 'Helps a lot, and NVIDIA cards are the best tested. Without one, models run on the processor, more slowly.' },
];
---

<SiteLayout>
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div class="hero-inner container">
      <p class="eyebrow hero-eyebrow"><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> Local-first. Open source. In development.</p>
      <img class="hero-logo" src="/eugene-transparent.svg" alt="Eugene, the glasses-wearing face surrounded by a network ring" width="152" height="152" fetchpriority="high" />
      <h1 id="hero-title">Eugene Plexus<span class="heading-period">.</span></h1>
      <p class="hero-statement" data-plain>Run AI models on <strong>your own computer.</strong></p>
      <p class="hero-description" data-plain>Private and free. Eugene sets up the engine, suggests a model that fits your machine, and gives your apps one address to use.</p>
      <p class="hero-technical">A self-hosted control plane for local LLM inference, with OpenAI and Anthropic Messages APIs.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/install"><ArrowDown size={19} aria-hidden="true" /> Install the alpha <ArrowRight size={17} aria-hidden="true" /></a>
        <a class="text-link" href="#uses">See what it can do <ArrowDown size={17} aria-hidden="true" /></a>
      </div>
      <p class="release-note">{release.version} is available for early testing. No stable release yet.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <nav class="pipeline-strip" aria-label="Explore the inference workflow">
    <div class="container pipeline-track">
      {stages.map((stage) => (
        <a href={`#${stage.id}`} class="pipeline-stop">
          <span class="stage-number">{stage.number}</span>
          <stage.icon size={21} stroke-width={1.6} aria-hidden="true" />
          <span>{stage.short}</span>
          <ArrowRight class="pipeline-arrow" size={16} aria-hidden="true" />
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </nav>

  <section class="section uses-section" id="uses" aria-labelledby="uses-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading" data-plain>
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-index">01 /</span> What you can do</p>
        <h2 id="uses-title">Your own AI,<br />on hardware you already have.</h2>
        <p>Four things people set it up for.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="uses" role="list" data-plain>
        {uses.map((use) => (
          <li class="use">
            <use.icon size={26} stroke-width={1.5} aria-hidden="true" />
            <h3>{use.title}</h3>
            <p>{use.text}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section see-section" id="see" aria-labelledby="see-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading" data-plain>
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-index">02 /</span> See it</p>
        <h2 id="see-title">This is what you get.</h2>
        <p>Real screens from a running install, with a small model answering on the processor.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="shots" data-plain>
        <figure class="shot shot-reply">
          <img src="/screenshots/first-reply.webp" width="1200" height="575" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="The Try it card in the console. A model named qwen3-0.6b has answered the question “Suggest three names for a friendly home robot.”" />
          <figcaption>Your first reply, right in the browser.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="shot shot-phone">
          <img src="/screenshots/phone-first-reply.webp" width="480" height="642" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="The same Try it card on a phone-sized screen, answering “What is a good name for a cat?” with “Whisker”." />
          <figcaption>The same page on your phone.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="shot shot-apps">
          <img src="/screenshots/connect-your-apps.webp" width="1200" height="432" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="The Use it from your apps card: one address, a model name, a key, and setup buttons for Claude Code, Continue, Cline, Open WebUI, SillyTavern, OpenCode, any OpenAI SDK and curl." />
          <figcaption>One address, a model name and a key for your apps, with setup steps for each.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section requirements-section" id="requirements" aria-labelledby="requirements-title">
    <div class="container requirements-layout">
      <div data-plain>
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-index">03 /</span> Before you install</p>
        <h2 id="requirements-title">Will it run on my computer?</h2>
        <p>If it has 16 GB of memory, probably. Eugene checks your real hardware before any download and tells you which models fit.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/install#before">Everything you need <ArrowRight size={17} aria-hidden="true" /></a>
      </div>
      <dl class="requirements" data-plain>
        {requirements.map((item) => (
          <div class="requirement">
            <dt>{item.term}</dt>
            <dd>{item.text}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="platform" aria-labelledby="platform-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-index">04 /</span> The platform</p>
        <h2 id="platform-title">Everything you run,<br />working together.</h2>
        <p>Start on one machine, with engines and model settings you can manage from a browser. Add the AI apps and services you already use, then more machines when you need them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="principles">
        <article class="principle">
          <Layers size={27} stroke-width={1.5} aria-hidden="true" />
          <h3>Engine setup, handled</h3>
          <p>Install and update llama.cpp from the browser, and restart it after a crash. Run a vLLM installation you already have. Eugene Plexus wraps upstream engines instead of replacing them.</p>
          <span class="principle-note">The operations layer, not another engine.</span>
        </article>
        <article class="principle">
          <SlidersHorizontal size={27} stroke-width={1.5} aria-hidden="true" />
          <h3>Settings start with your machine</h3>
          <p>Get a suggested model and a memory estimate before you download. Settings start at a conversation length that fits the machine you picked, and you can change them.</p>
          <span class="principle-note">A fit estimate, not a speed guarantee.</span>
        </article>
        <article class="principle">
          <HardDrive size={27} stroke-width={1.5} aria-hidden="true" />
          <h3>Your files stay yours</h3>
          <p>Find and download models into your own folders as plainly named files, or scan the models already there. Remove Eugene Plexus and your original model files stay where you put them.</p>
          <span class="principle-note">Your library, without the lock-in.</span>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section workflow-section" id="workflow" aria-labelledby="workflow-title">
    <div class="container workflow-layout">
      <div class="workflow-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-index">05 /</span> The workflow</p>
        <h2 id="workflow-title">Your models.<br />Your engines.<br /><span class="accent-text">One place to run them.</span></h2>
        <p>From a model on disk to a service your tools can use.</p>
        <p>The console's Home page brings setup, the first model, and the first reply together. The side panel keeps every machine and setting within reach as your setup grows.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/architecture">Explore the architecture <ArrowRight size={17} aria-hidden="true" /></a>
      </div>
      <ol class="workflow-stages">
        {stages.map((stage) => (
          <li class="workflow-stage" id={stage.id}>
            <span class="workflow-number" aria-hidden="true">{stage.number}</span>
            <div class="workflow-stage-content">
              <div class="stage-heading"><h3>{stage.name}</h3><stage.icon size={23} stroke-width={1.5} aria-hidden="true" /></div>
              <p>{stage.description}</p>
              <div class="stage-meta">
                <span><Check size={14} aria-hidden="true" />{stage.output}</span>
                <a href={`https://github.com/eugene-plexus/${stage.repo}`} aria-label={`Explore the ${stage.repo} source for ${stage.short.toLowerCase()}`}>Source <ArrowUpRight size={14} aria-hidden="true" /></a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section open-source-section" id="open-source" aria-labelledby="open-source-title">
    <div class="container open-source-layout">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-index">06 /</span> Built in the open</p>
        <h2 id="open-source-title">Early days.<br />An open invitation.</h2>
        <p>Eugene Plexus is under active development. The current alpha is available for early testing, with versioned installers and a matching container image. There is no stable release yet.</p>
        <p>Follow the <a href="https://github.com/eugene-plexus/specs/blob/main/docs/design/adoption-roadmap.md">current roadmap</a> and <a href="https://github.com/eugene-plexus/specs/tree/main/docs/acceptance">acceptance records</a> for what is built, what has been exercised, and what still needs work.</p>
        <a class="button button-primary" href="https://github.com/eugene-plexus">Find us on GitHub <ArrowUpRight size={18} aria-hidden="true" /></a>
      </div>
      <div class="project-facts">
        <div class="fact"><span>Project status</span><strong class="status"><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> In development</strong></div>
        <div class="fact"><span>Core license</span><a href="https://github.com/eugene-plexus/specs/blob/main/LICENSE">Apache 2.0 <ArrowUpRight size={15} aria-hidden="true" /></a></div>
        <div class="fact"><span>Built for</span><strong>Local LLM inference</strong></div>
        <div class="fact"><span>Development</span><a href="https://github.com/orgs/eugene-plexus/repositories">Public repositories <ArrowUpRight size={15} aria-hidden="true" /></a></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section questions-section" id="questions" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
    <div class="container questions-layout">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="section-index">07 /</span> A few details</p>
        <h2 id="questions-title">Good questions.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="questions">
        <details data-plain>
          <summary>Is it free?<ChevronDown size={19} aria-hidden="true" /></summary>
          <p>Yes. Eugene Plexus is open source under the Apache 2.0 license. There is no account, subscription or usage fee. If you choose to connect a paid cloud service, that service bills you as it normally would.</p>
        </details>
        <details data-plain>
          <summary>Does anything I type leave my computer?<ChevronDown size={19} aria-hidden="true" /></summary>
          <p>Not when you use a model on your own machine. Your messages go from your app to that machine and back. Eugene itself goes online to find and download models from Hugging Face, and to download or update the llama.cpp engine from GitHub. If you add a cloud service such as OpenAI, messages sent to it go to that service. A key marked local-only never falls back to one.</p>
        </details>
        <details data-plain>
          <summary>How is this different from Ollama, LM Studio or ChatGPT?<ChevronDown size={19} aria-hidden="true" /></summary>
          <p>ChatGPT runs in someone else's data center. Eugene runs models on hardware you own. Ollama and LM Studio also run models on your computer, and you can keep them. Eugene can use them beside the models it runs, with one address for all your apps. What it adds is the care around the engine: installing and updating it, suggesting a model that fits, and restarting it after a crash. You can also reach it from your other devices, and add more machines later.</p>
        </details>
        <details data-plain>
          <summary>Do I need to use the command line?<ChevronDown size={19} aria-hidden="true" /></summary>
          <p>Only to install. On Windows or Linux, installing is one command you paste and run. After that, everything happens in your web browser: choosing a model, downloading it, running it and connecting your apps. The Docker and NAS option takes a few more steps.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is this a chatbot or an inference engine?<ChevronDown size={19} aria-hidden="true" /></summary>
          <p>Neither. Eugene Plexus is the control plane around your inference setup. It installs and supervises upstream engines, manages model files and launch settings, and routes requests. The console's Home page offers a first reply; the playground is a diagnostic client, not a replacement for your chat or coding app.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I download it yet?<ChevronDown size={19} aria-hidden="true" /></summary>
          <p>Yes. <a href="/install">Install {release.version}</a> to help test the current alpha. The guide covers setup, your first reply, updates and removal. This is an early testing release; see the <a href="/architecture#status">current boundaries</a> before relying on it.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which inference engines does it support?<ChevronDown size={19} aria-hidden="true" /></summary>
          <p>The current integrations install and supervise llama.cpp and drive user-installed vLLM. Development builds after alpha.2 add experimental MLX support for Apple silicon, not yet checked on a physical Mac, and experimental Kev decision models; neither is in alpha.2. Existing OpenAI-compatible servers, including Ollama and LM Studio, can also be connected without Eugene Plexus managing their processes. Hardware verification remains uneven; see the <a href="/architecture#status">current boundaries</a>.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I use Claude Code and my other tools?<ChevronDown size={19} aria-hidden="true" /></summary>
          <p>The gateway serves OpenAI-compatible chat, tool calling and embeddings, plus Anthropic Messages for text and tool calls. Client keys can be named and revoked from the console. Claude Code edit/check tasks and Open WebUI chat and images have been exercised with actual local models; full vendor API parity is not claimed. See <a href="/architecture#status">compatibility limits</a>.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I manage more than one machine?<ChevronDown size={19} aria-hidden="true" /></summary>
          <p>Yes. Enroll nodes and manage them from one authenticated console, including remote settings and folder browsing. A Library folder can describe where other machines mount it. Each node can optionally keep a local copy of the models it runs, while leaving the original files alone. Use a trusted LAN or a mesh VPN such as Tailscale for access from other devices.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How can I get involved?<ChevronDown size={19} aria-hidden="true" /></summary>
          <p>Browse the <a href="https://github.com/orgs/eugene-plexus/repositories">GitHub repositories</a>, read a component's contributing guide, and open an issue with a bug report or an idea. Contributions use the Developer Certificate of Origin, with signed-off commits.</p>
        </details>
        <p class="ask" data-plain><Face size={40} /><span>Still have a question? <a href={askUrl}>Ask it on GitHub</a>. You need a free GitHub account, and no question is too basic.</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
